( ============================================================
  Call-stack trace test #1: BRK INSIDE a nested function.

  Call chain:  main -> outer -> inner
  The BRK (our breakpoint) sits INSIDE `inner`, before it returns.

  Expected LIVE call stack at the BRK:   MAIN, outer, inner
  Expected working-stack value at BRK:    0006   ( 2 +1 +3 )

  Why this matters:
  A BRK at the end of main would show only [MAIN], because by then
  outer and inner have already returned. Putting the BRK *inside*
  inner is what makes the call path visible -- exactly like setting
  a gdb breakpoint deep in the call chain and typing `bt`.
  ============================================================ )

|0100 @main
    #0002 ;outer JSR2     ( call outer with n = 2 )
    #18 DEO               ( never reached: BRK in inner halts first )
    BRK

@outer ( n -- n2 )
    #0001 ADD2            ( n + 1 )
    ;inner JSR2           ( call inner )
    JMP2r

@inner ( n -- n2 )
    #0003 ADD2            ( n + 3 )
    BRK                   ( <-- breakpoint deep in the call chain )
    JMP2r
